.TH CVEC_SORT 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_sort \- sort a vector with the default comparison for its type
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_sort(v);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Sorts the vector in place with the default comparison function for the
element type, which comes from the csort library; the type decides which
comparator is used.

A char* vector can hold NULL elements. The default string comparison puts
NULL before every non-NULL string and treats NULL as equal only to
another NULL, so all NULL elements end up at the front of the sorted
vector and the other elements follow in strcmp() order.

An element type with no default comparison procedure is a compile-time
error rather than a run-time one. Whether a type has a default comparison
procedure depends on the type alone, so cvec_sort carries a
_Static_assert and such a call never builds. The diagnostic names the
vector and points at cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc.

These element types sort: every standard integer type in both
signednesses, bool (false before true), every enumerated type, float,
double, long double, char*, const char*, signed char* and unsigned
char*. Each of the char pointer types sorts as a NUL-terminated string in
strcmp() order, and uint8_t* is an unsigned char* that sorts the same
way. Sort a vector of pointers to binary buffers with
cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc and a comparator that compares the
pointers. A char pointer that is itself const or volatile, such as
const char *const, also sorts, by the content of its string. An
enumerated type is compatible with one of the standard integer types, and
the macro uses the comparison procedure of that type.

The types refused at compile time are the ones with no default
comparison procedure: a struct, a union, a pointer to anything other than
char, and a fixed-size char array such as char[64]. A vector of an enum
type isn't one of them and sorts normally. Sort a refused element type
with cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc and an explicit comparison
procedure.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v    Vector to sort
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The sort is an in-place, stable mergesort that moves the elements inside
the backing array directly, with no call per element access. It takes
O(n log n) time and O(n) space and uses the default comparison for the
element type. A NULL char* element is ordered without being
dereferenced.

The code doesn't compile when the element type has no default comparison
procedure; the diagnostic names the vector and points at
cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc. cvec_find differs on purpose: it
accepts every element type and falls back to byte-wise equality, because
equality can come from the bytes of an element but an order can't.

When the internal temporary buffer of the sort can't be allocated, the
macro calls _ccol_fatal_err(), as every other type-inferred macro in
cvector.h that changes a vector does (cvec_push, cvec_reserve,
cvec_append_array and the others). The vector is then left completely
unsorted.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_push(vec, 3);
cvec_push(vec, 1);
cvec_push(vec, 2);
cvec_sort(vec);  // vec is now [1, 2, 3]
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc (3),
.BR cvec_find (3),
.BR csort_get_default_comparison_proc (3)
